{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"call-apply-bind","title":"call, apply & bind","group":"Functions & Scope","order":18,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Three methods on every function that let you choose <code>this</code> explicitly — invoke now (<code>call</code>, <code>apply</code>) or later (<code>bind</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Invokes immediately?</th><th>Arguments</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>fn.call(thisArg, a, b)</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Listed one by one</td><td>The function's result</td></tr><tr><td><code>fn.apply(thisArg, [a, b])</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>As an array</td><td>The function's result</td></tr><tr><td><code>fn.bind(thisArg, a)</code></td><td>No</td><td>Pre-filled (partial application)</td><td>A new bound function</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function introduce(greeting, punctuation) {\n  return `${greeting}, I'm ${this.name}${punctuation}`;\n}\nconst asha = { name: \"Asha\" };\nconsole.log(introduce.call(asha, \"Hello\", \"!\"));\nconsole.log(introduce.apply(asha, [\"Hi\", \".\"]));\nconst boundHey = introduce.bind(asha, \"Hey\");\nconsole.log(boundHey(\"?\"));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Hello, I'm Asha!\nHi, I'm Asha.\nHey, I'm Asha?</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tool\"></i>Classic uses</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>// borrow array methods for array-likes\nfunction joinArgs() {\n  return Array.prototype.join.call(arguments, \"-\");\n}\nconsole.log(joinArgs(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"));\n\n// apply spreads an array (pre-ES6 alternative to Math.max(...nums))\nconsole.log(Math.max.apply(null, [3, 7, 2]));\n\n// partial application with bind\nconst multiply = (a, b) =&gt; a * b;\nconst double = multiply.bind(null, 2);\nconsole.log(double(21));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>a-b-c\n7\n42</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>bind is permanent</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function whoAmI() { return this.id; }\nconst bound = whoAmI.bind({ id: \"first\" });\nconst rebound = bound.bind({ id: \"second\" });\nconsole.log(bound(), rebound(), bound.call({ id: \"third\" }));\nconsole.log(bound.name);\n\nconst arrow = () =&gt; this;\nconsole.log(arrow.call({ id: 1 }));   // arrows ignore call/apply/bind for this</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>first first first\nbound whoAmI\nundefined</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>new</code> beats <code>bind</code>: calling a bound function with <code>new</code> ignores the bound <code>this</code> (but keeps pre-filled arguments).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between call, apply and bind?</summary><div><p>All three set <code>this</code> explicitly. <code>call</code> invokes immediately with arguments listed individually; <code>apply</code> invokes immediately with arguments as an array; <code>bind</code> doesn't invoke — it returns a new function with <code>this</code> (and optionally leading arguments) permanently fixed.</p></div></details></section>"}